"Distribute the positive integers into three columns $C_1$, $C_2$ and $C_3$ by residue class modulo $3$, and let $t_n^\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{N}^*$ denote the integer in column $C_n$ and row $\\alpha$. Let $\\mathbb{P}$ be the set of positive prime numbers, ordered as $p_1=2,p_2=3,p_3=5,\\dots$, and let $p_m^\\delta$ denote the prime in the $m$-th prime column and row $\\delta$. For a positive even integer $t_n^\\alpha$, the conjecture specifies a representation using one prime in the corresponding prime column and another prime.\n\n**Indexed strong Goldbach representation conjecture.** Let $t_n^\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{N}^*$ be a positive even integer. Then there esist two prime numbers $p_{n}^{\\delta(\\alpha)}, \\,p_{m}^{\\gamma}\\in\\mathbb{P}$ such that\n\n$$\nt_{n}^{\\alpha}=p_{n}^{\\delta(\\alpha)}+p_{m}^{\\gamma}.\n$$\n\nThis is presented as a reformulation of the strong Goldbach conjecture in the paper's column-and-row notation. Its status therefore tracks the unresolved strong Goldbach problem rather than constituting an independent result."